        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Toronto Maple Leafs aren't just reshaping their roster; they're betting on a legend to anchor a new era. <strong>Sergei Bobrovsky, the two-time Stanley Cup champion and Vezina Trophy winner, has signed a three-year, $21 million contract with the Leafs</strong>, a bold move by new GM John Chayka to overhaul a team that finished second-to-last in the Eastern Conference with 78 points. At 37, Bobrovsky isn't chasing youth; he's embracing a \"legendary, historical team,\" even ceding No. 72 to top prospect Gavin McKenna to wear it himself. Alongside Anthony Stolarz, the tandem mirrors the Panthers' 2024 Cup-winning duo, signaling a shift from overreliance on stars to veteran depth. With Duhaime, Blueger, and Raddysh also added, the Leafs are building for now. <strong>What to watch: How Bobrovsky's presence elevates a young core and whether Chayka's aggressive spending translates to a deeper playoff run</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated July 3, 2026"
